{
	/*
		Node tool configs type-check against the lowest runtime allowed by root `devEngines`
		(currently ^22.22.2 || ^24.15.0 || >=26.0.0, so the floor is 22.22.2). An API that
		exists only on a newer allowed line waits until that floor includes it.

		`target` is the newest edition whose syntax the floor implements. `lib` includes that
		edition, then any later finished group in which every declared API exists on the floor.

		A finished group is a yearly `ES20xx` or `ES20xx.*` library. TypeScript names it for the
		edition that standardized it, which can be years after the engine shipped the API, and a
		Node.js backport can move an API onto an older line. `ESNext.*` groups stay out: TypeScript
		adds declarations to them in later releases, so a group that matches the floor today can
		name an API the floor never receives. Recheck this list when `devEngines` changes, when
		Node.js backports one of these APIs onto the floor, and when a TypeScript upgrade moves
		an untyped API into a finished group.

		On the current floor, past ES2024:
		- ES2025.Iterator — iterator helpers, since Node.js 22.0.0.
		- ES2025.Collection — Set methods such as `union`, since Node.js 22.0.0.

		On the floor, but untyped. TypeScript 7 exposes `Array.fromAsync` (Node.js 22.0.0) only
		through `ESNext.Array`.

		Still above the floor:
		- Node.js 23: ES2025.Intl (`Intl.DurationFormat`), ES2025.Promise (`Promise.try`).
		- Node.js 24: ES2025.RegExp (`RegExp.escape`), ES2025.Float16 (`Float16Array`,
			`Math.f16round`), ESNext.Disposable (`using`, `DisposableStack`; Node.js 22's
			`Symbol.dispose` covers only `fs` and `stream`), ESNext.Error (`Error.isError`),
			ESNext.SharedMemory (`Atomics.pause`).
		- Node.js 25: ESNext.TypedArrays (`Uint8Array` base64 and hex conversions).
		- Node.js 26: ESNext.Collection (`Map.getOrInsert`, `WeakMap.getOrInsert`),
			ESNext.Date (`Date.toTemporalInstant`), ESNext.Temporal, and ESNext.Intl. The Locale
			methods in that last group (`getCalendars` and the rest) are methods from Node.js 24;
			Node.js 22 still exposes them as accessors, and the same file also types Temporal
			arguments on `Intl.DateTimeFormat`.
		- ESNext.Decorators (`Symbol.metadata`) has no Node.js release.
	*/
	"extends": "./tsconfig.base.json",
	"compilerOptions": {
		"target": "ES2024",
		"lib": ["ES2024", "ES2025.Iterator", "ES2025.Collection"],
		"types": ["node"]
	},
	// Not used on its own. Root and `packages/configs/tsconfig.json` extend this and set `include`.
	"files": []
}
